    "question": "Write a Python class `SimpleRunnablePipe` that supports the `|` operator: chaining two functions `step1 | step2` so that calling `pipe.invoke(x)` returns `step2(step1(x))`.",
    "starterCode": "class SimpleRunnablePipe:\n    # your code here\n    pass",
    "expectedOutput": "pipe.invoke(5) == 12",
    "solution": "class SimpleRunnablePipe:\n    def __init__(self, func):\n        self.func = func\n    def __or__(self, other):\n        if isinstance(other, SimpleRunnablePipe):\n            return SimpleRunnablePipe(lambda x: other.invoke(self.invoke(x)))\n        return SimpleRunnablePipe(lambda x: other(self.invoke(x)))\n    def invoke(self, x):\n        return self.func(x)",
